It was shown in Lemma 3.1 that (6.51) enters into that class of problems with the following correspondence:

\[X=H^{1}_{0}(\Omega)^{N}\times L^{2}_{0}(\Omega),\quad\ Y=H^{-1}(\Omega)^{N},\quad \ T=\mbox{the Stokes operator},\]

\[\lambda=1/v,\quad\ G(\lambda,u)=\lambda\biggl{(}\sum_{j=1}^{N}u_{j}(\partial \mathbf{u}/\partial x_{j})-\mathbf{f}\biggr{)}.\]

Moreover, \((\mathbf{u},p)\) is a solution of (6.51) iff \(u=(\mathbf{u},p/v)\) is a solution of (6.48). Then Newton's algorithm (6.49) reads:

